# MVS 3.8j TK5 (Update 5) -- TSO logon through ISPF and back out. CANONICAL FORM.
#
# DERIVED, NOT RECORDED. This is `packages/fixtures/mvs/mvs-tk5-tso-ispf.trace`
# converted to the trace format `parseTrace` accepts. Regenerate with:
#
#   sed -n -e '/^#/p' -e 's/^data: \([0-9.]\+ [<>=+]\)/\1/p' \
#       packages/fixtures/mvs/mvs-tk5-tso-ispf.trace \
#     > packages/fixtures/traces/mvs-tk5-tso-ispf.trace
#
# (then restore this header, which replaces the original's -- see REDACTION below).
#
# WHY THE CONVERSION EXISTS. The raw file is CLI stdout: every line carries a
# `data: ` prefix, and interleaved with the protocol are ScreenText dumps and OIA
# status lines. `parseTrace` (core/src/trace.ts) matches `/^([0-9.]+)\s+([<>=+])\s*(.*)$/`
# after a trim, so NOT ONE line of the raw file matches it. Replaying the raw file
# does not error -- it silently yields an empty unformatted screen: 0 fields, 0
# attributes, 1920 uniformly green cells. Stripping the six-character prefix is the
# whole of the fix; the 895 protocol lines and their bytes are otherwise untouched.
#
# BOTH FILES ARE KEPT ON PURPOSE and are not redundant. The raw one is the full
# capture record: it holds the rendered ScreenText panels and the OIA lines, which
# document what the host actually displayed and which this file necessarily drops.
# This one is the replayable protocol subset. If you re-record, re-derive this.
#
# What this proves, and what it does not:
#   * ISPF primary option menu reached (USERID: HERC02, TERMINAL: 3277), then a
#     clean logoff back to the VTAM Logon panel. 0 errors, 0 program checks.
#   * TERMINAL: 3277 and the status lines' "24 80" confirm the session stayed
#     24x80 throughout. TSO did NOT need a larger screen, and never asked for
#     one -- the 27x132 in the earlier zti capture was zti advertising its own
#     window size, not a TSO requirement.
#   * The three-unit Query Reply (Summary, Usable Area, Implicit Partition) was
#     ACCEPTED by this host.
#   * SET ATTRIBUTE IS REAL HERE, AND THIS IS THE ONLY COMMITTED EVIDENCE OF IT.
#     113 SA orders across the session -- 101 of type X'42' (foreground colour)
#     and 12 of type X'00' (reset character attributes) -- and 0 MF orders. Those
#     counts come from the parser, never a hex grep; see
#     packages/cli/scripts/count-orders.mjs and its header for why a grep is wrong
#     in both directions. Replayed, this yields 532 of 1920 cells carrying a
#     character-level foreground, resolving to five distinct colours. Every one of
#     those 113 orders was PARSED AND DISCARDED for the whole life of the project
#     until the TUI/colour work; render.test.ts now pins it.
#   * WHAT IT CANNOT PROVE: THERE ARE ZERO SFE ORDERS IN THIS CAPTURE. All 113 SA
#     orders are character-level, so no field-attribute cell here carries extended
#     attributes and the FIELD-LEVEL fallback -- resolve()'s second precedence
#     level -- gets no coverage from real host traffic at all. That is precisely
#     why that class of defect went unnoticed. Real coverage needs a capture from
#     a host that sends SFE; none is committed. render.test.ts asserts the zero, so
#     that test failing is the signal to go and add field-level coverage.
#
# REDACTION. The password appears TWICE in a raw capture of this session and both
# are already removed upstream -- once as EBCDIC on the wire (overwritten with six
# X'00' bytes, hence the run of nulls in the outbound LOGON record) and once as
# PLAINTEXT, because MVS 3.8j echoes the password to the screen with no masking, in
# a ScreenText dump that this file drops entirely. Verified on this file: zero
# occurrences of the password's EBCDIC bytes in the reassembled wire stream, in
# either casing, and zero ScreenText lines.
#
# THE HEADER ITSELF NO LONGER SPELLS THE PASSWORD, which is the one way this file
# differs from the raw one beyond the prefix. The original header quoted both the
# plaintext password and its EBCDIC byte string inside its own redaction note, so a
# grep for either hit the documentation and could never come back clean -- and a
# check that always fires is not a check. The credential is a published TK5
# default (doc/MVS_TK4-_v100_Users_Manual.pdf) and is named in docs/live-testing.md
# for whoever needs to re-record; it does not need to be in a fixture too.
#
# Log off properly or the next run draws IKJ56425I LOGON REJECTED ... IN USE, which
# is NOT the IKT00405I failure and has been misscored as one before.
